diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase sweep of the ospf_enterprise_static topology found no fault at any layer. Phase 1 (get_reachability) showed 0 % ICMP loss on every sampled path, including host_1_1_1_1 → web_server_0 (10.200.0.3) and web_server_1 (10.200.0.4). Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL drop rules, no MAC address conflicts, no static ARP poisoning, no addressing or gateway divergence, and all host default routes present. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed all six FRR routers — router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3, switch_dist_1_1, switch_dist_2_1, and switch_server_access — are running ospfd with vtysh reachable, all OSPF adjacencies in the Full/DR or Full/Backup state, 10 OSPF routes installed on each router, and zero traffic-control shaping anywhere in the fabric. Phase D (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) showed host_1_1_1_1 correctly resolving all four web hostnames via the dns_server (10.200.0.2) and receiving HTTP 200 responses from all four web servers (web0.local through web3.local) in under 6 ms each. No stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion were found on any device. The user's report of "completely lost access" since a maintenance window is not supported by any current evidence or historical trace: there is no flap script or process that could cause link instability, no OSPF adjacency degradation, no ACL blocking traffic, and no crashed service. Root cause: the reported outage is unconfirmed — no fault is present in the live topology. No device needs to be restarted; the recommended action is to re-verify the client-side configuration on the reporting workstation (e.g., host routing table, local firewall, or VPN client) rather than touching any lab device.
=== END ===